{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the left-most (most significant) unset bit in a given integer.\n    If no unset bits are found within the number's effective bit length,\n    the original number is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle the special case where the number is 0.\n    # For 0, the left-most unset bit is at position 0, setting it results in 1.\n    if number == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Determine the number of bits required to represent the integer.\n    # For positive numbers, bit_length() gives the position of the highest set bit + 1.\n    num_bits = number.bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from the highest bit position down to 0.\n    # The highest bit position is num_bits - 1.\n    for i in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the bit at the current position 'i' is unset (0).\n        # We use a bitmask (1 << i) and bitwise AND.\n        if not (number & (1 << i)):\n            # If the bit is unset, set it by performing a bitwise OR with the bitmask.\n            return number | (1 << i)\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits up to num_bits - 1 are already set.\n    # As per the design context, if no unset bit is found within this range,\n    # the original number should be returned.\n    return number"}
